## The question you're answering

A national supermarket chain runs an online application portal for
management trainee roles. As part of the application, every applicant
must complete a numerical-reasoning test with a fixed pass mark.
Applicants whose first language is not English score on average 30%
lower than native English speakers on the test, even when they have
equivalent qualifications and prior work experience. The supermarket
says the test measures abilities the role requires.

Discuss whether the supermarket's use of the test is lawful under the
Equality Act 2010. (4 marks)

The previous worked example (M.4.1) taught you to anchor the Name on
the actor and what they did, not on the data in the scenario. This
one teaches the next failure pattern most often seen on Paper 1
legislation answers: naming the Act correctly, explaining the
disadvantage correctly, and then stopping. On indirect-discrimination
questions in particular, the Impact-level marks live in a place most
students do not look. They live in the justification defence.

Section 19 of the Equality Act 2010 makes indirect discrimination
prima facie unlawful. A provision, criterion or practice that applies
to everyone but puts a protected group at a particular disadvantage
is indirect discrimination. The student answers below all spot this
correctly. None of them earn full marks, because Paper 1
indirect-discrimination questions are not asking "is this a
disadvantage?". They are asking "can the practice be justified?".

The justification gate is at section 19(2)(d). The practice is lawful
if it is a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim. The
question always turns on whether the gate is satisfied, not on
whether the disadvantage exists.

As in the earlier worked examples, the answers below are highlighted
to show structure inline. The {% role name="name" %}Name{% /role %} is
tinted green, the {% role name="explain" %}Explain{% /role %} is
tinted gold, and the {% role name="impact" %}Impact{% /role %} is
tinted orange. A missing colour means the corresponding component is
absent or earns no mark.

## Answer One: stops at the disadvantage

> {% role name="name" %}The supermarket has breached the Equality Act 2010. The test is indirect discrimination against applicants whose first language is not English.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}Indirect discrimination occurs when a provision, criterion or practice that applies to everyone puts a protected group at a particular disadvantage. The numerical-reasoning test is the practice. It applies to all applicants equally. Race is a protected characteristic, and nationality and first language are linked to race under the Act. The 30% gap shows that the test puts non-native English speakers at a substantial disadvantage compared with native English speakers.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}The supermarket's hiring process is therefore unlawful. Affected applicants can bring claims to the employment tribunal, where the supermarket faces compensation orders and reputational damage from being publicly named as a discriminator.{% /role %}

A confident, structured answer that uses the language the mark scheme
is looking for. It names the Act, it names the right form of
discrimination, it identifies the protected characteristic, it names
the disadvantage and quantifies it. Three of the four marks look like
they should be there. Read the question again before assuming so.

**Name.** "The Equality Act 2010, indirect discrimination." Correct
and specific enough for the Name mark. The student has done the
diagnostic move that M.4.1 taught: anchored on the actor (the
supermarket) and the act (applying a test that filters applicants).
Full Name mark earned.

**Explain.** The mechanism of indirect discrimination is spelled out
correctly. Provision-criterion-practice is named, the protected
characteristic is identified, the disadvantage is described and the
30% figure is used as evidence. The student has clearly understood
what indirect discrimination means and how to read a scenario for
its components. Full Explain mark earned.

**Impact.** This is where the answer collapses. "Unlawful, therefore
tribunal claims" is not the impact the question is asking for. It is
the impact that would follow *if* the practice has already been
established as unlawful. But on an indirect-discrimination scenario
under section 19, establishing the disadvantage is only the first
half of the analysis. The second half is section 19(2)(d): the
practice is lawful if the employer can show it is a proportionate
means of achieving a legitimate aim. The student has not engaged
with this. The whole question of whether the supermarket has actually
breached the Act is left undecided. The Impact paragraph asserts an
outcome the analysis has not earned. Zero marks for Impact.

Marks this would earn: 2 out of 4. Strong Name and Explain, missing
Impact because the question's actual hinge has not been touched.

The diagnostic worth taking from Answer One is that on indirect-
discrimination scenarios, the disadvantage paragraph is setup, not
conclusion. The question is asking what happens *after* the
disadvantage is established. If the answer treats the disadvantage as
sufficient to conclude unlawfulness, the answer has stopped at
section 19(1) and missed section 19(2)(d).

## Answer Two: mentions justification but does not apply it

> {% role name="name" %}The supermarket has potentially breached the Equality Act 2010 through indirect discrimination under section 19, although it may have a defence.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}Indirect discrimination occurs where a provision, criterion or practice puts a protected group at a particular disadvantage. The numerical-reasoning test is the practice; it applies to all applicants equally, but non-native English speakers score 30% lower than native English speakers, which is a substantial disadvantage. Race is a protected characteristic and is linked to first language. The supermarket would need to justify the practice in order for it to be lawful.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}If the supermarket cannot justify the practice, the affected applicants can bring claims to the employment tribunal and the supermarket faces compensation and reputational damage. If the supermarket can justify it, the practice is lawful.{% /role %}

A more cautious answer than Answer One. The student has registered
that there is a justification defence and has named it. Let's see
what the marker thinks.

**Name.** "Equality Act 2010, indirect discrimination under section
19, with a defence." The section number is named, the defence is
named in headline. Full Name mark earned.

**Explain.** The disadvantage is still well-explained. The student has
added a sentence about justification ("would need to justify the
practice") but has not said anything about *what* justification means
or how it would be applied to the scenario. The Explain mark is still
earned because the disadvantage half of the section 19 analysis is
complete; the justification half is named but not developed.

**Impact.** This is the problem. "If justified, lawful; if not,
unlawful" is not an answer to the question. The student has
recognised the gate exists and then walked away from running through
it. The whole point of the question is to run the gate against the
specific facts of the scenario. What is the legitimate aim the
supermarket is pursuing? Is the test a proportionate means of
achieving it? Without engaging with those two limbs, the Impact
paragraph contains no actual analysis. It is a conditional with
nothing in either branch. Markers see this kind of answer often, and
the mark scheme typically credits it half a mark to nothing,
depending on the question's specific weighting. We will mark it
generously here as one mark, on the basis that the student has at
least recognised the gate exists.

Marks this would earn: 3 out of 4. Strong Name and Explain, partial
Impact because the gate is named but not applied.

The diagnostic worth taking from Answer Two is that *naming* the
justification defence is not the same as *running* it. Recognising
that section 19(2)(d) exists is the work of half a sentence and
earns less than half a mark. Running the two-limb test (legitimate
aim, proportionate means) against the scenario is the work of an
Impact paragraph and earns the rest.

## Answer Three: the full four marks

> {% role name="name" %}The supermarket's practice engages section 19 of the Equality Act 2010 (indirect discrimination), with the question turning on the section 19(2)(d) justification defence.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}The numerical-reasoning test is a provision, criterion or practice that applies uniformly to all applicants. It puts applicants whose first language is not English at a particular disadvantage compared with native English speakers, evidenced by the 30% score gap. Race is a protected characteristic, and nationality and first language are sufficiently linked to race for the protected-group requirement to be met. The first half of section 19 is therefore satisfied. The practice is prima facie unlawful unless the supermarket can show, under section 19(2)(d), that the test is a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}Whether the practice is lawful turns on two questions. First, what is the legitimate aim? The supermarket says the test measures abilities the role requires. If the management trainee role genuinely requires numerical reasoning of the kind the test measures, the aim is legitimate. Second, is the test a proportionate means of achieving that aim? Proportionality requires that no less-discriminatory alternative would do the job equally well. If the role actually requires only basic arithmetic, a sophisticated numerical-reasoning test is disproportionate, because a simpler test would measure the relevant ability with a smaller disadvantage to non-native English speakers. On the facts as given (a 30% gap, no evidence of role-specific calibration), the test is likely to be disproportionate, and the practice unlawful. Affected applicants would have claims at the employment tribunal, with compensation calculated on injury to feelings plus lost opportunity.{% /role %}

Walk through it.

**Name.** Both section 19 and section 19(2)(d) named. Justification
flagged as the operative gate, not as a footnote. The marker reads
this and sees a student who has identified that the question's centre
of gravity is the defence, not the disadvantage. Full Name mark
earned.

**Explain.** The first half of the section 19 analysis is complete
(provision-criterion-practice, protected characteristic,
disadvantage), and ends with the bridge to section 19(2)(d). The
Explain paragraph closes by stating that the practice is prima facie
unlawful *unless* the defence is made out. This is the move that
sets up the Impact paragraph to do real work rather than reach for
foregone conclusions. Full Explain mark earned.

**Impact.** The two-limb test is run against the scenario. Limb one
(legitimate aim): the supermarket's stated aim is named and tested
against the role. Limb two (proportionate means): the
less-discriminatory-alternative test is named and applied to the
specific facts. The conclusion (likely unlawful on these facts)
follows from the analysis rather than preceding it. The tribunal
consequences are tied to the conclusion. Full Impact mark earned.

Marks this would earn: 4 out of 4.

## What to take from all three

The lesson here is that section 19 is a two-step rule and Paper 1
questions on it are weighted toward the second step. Students lose
marks not because they fail to spot the disadvantage but because
they treat the disadvantage as the conclusion. The question is
asking what happens at the section 19(2)(d) gate.

Before you write the Impact paragraph on any indirect-discrimination
scenario, run this check:

1. Have I identified the legitimate aim the employer is pursuing?
2. Have I asked whether the practice is a proportionate means of
   achieving it?
3. Is there a less-discriminatory alternative I should name?

If the answer to any of these is no, your Impact paragraph is not
yet engaging with the question the examiner asked. It is engaging
with the question that section 19(1) asks (is there a disadvantage),
which the Explain paragraph has already answered.

A related trap, which earns no marks even when spotted, is naming
the defence in passing ("the employer would need to justify it") and
moving on. Markers credit the Name mark for naming the defence, but
the Impact mark is reserved for *running* the defence against the
specific facts. Hedging on the conclusion ("if justified, lawful; if
not, unlawful") is a confession that the second-limb work has not
been done.

One last note on indirect discrimination scenarios specifically.
Direct discrimination under section 13 generally has no justification
defence (with the narrow exception of direct age discrimination,
where section 13(2) does allow proportionality). Indirect
discrimination under section 19 always has the section 19(2)(d)
defence. If you confuse the two, you will either run a defence that
does not exist (and get marked down for it) or skip one that does
(and miss the Impact mark). The diagnostic move is: did the practice
treat the protected group differently *because of* the characteristic
(direct), or did it apply uniformly but disadvantage them in effect
(indirect)? The supermarket's test is the second pattern, so section
19 with the justification defence is the operative rule.

Run this check on every Paper 1 Equality Act answer you write,
before you commit. If your Impact paragraph treats the disadvantage
as the answer, you have stopped at section 19(1) and missed the part
of the question worth the most marks.
